---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---
<Layout
  title="Where to Swim Near Azizi Riviera"
  description="Looking for where to actually swim near Azizi Riviera? The honest answer: the building pool first, then a drive to the sea — the canal itself isn't a swim spot."
>
  <section class="container hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Water</p>
    <h1>Where to actually swim</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      If you searched for this because you want to know where to swim
      near Riviera, the short answer is: your building's pool, or a drive
      to the sea. The canal that runs along the development isn't a
      swimming spot, despite how the listing photos make it look.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container">
    <h2>Your building's pool</h2>
    <p>
      The realistic day-to-day answer. Most Riviera buildings have a
      shared residents' pool as a standard amenity — see the
      <a href="/pools/">pools page</a> for what's typical and how it varies
      by building. For most residents, this covers actual swimming far more
      than the canal or a beach trip does.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container">
    <h2>Why not the canal</h2>
    <p>
      The Dubai Water Canal is active boat and water-taxi traffic, not a
      designated or safe swimming area — it's infrastructure Riviera sits
      alongside, not a beach substitute, whatever the marketing renders
      suggest. Treat the canal walkway as a place to walk and run, not swim.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container">
    <h2>The actual sea</h2>
    <p>
      Kite Beach, La Mer, and JBR are the nearest public beaches, roughly a
      20–30 minute drive depending on traffic and where in Riviera you're
      starting from. There's no direct transit link — a car or taxi is the
      realistic way to get there for an actual sea swim.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>

